<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salagama</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chalia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Salagama are a Sinhalese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> of the south-western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, traditionally associated with weaving and later with peeling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">cinnamon</a>. Under the Portuguese and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, who drew great revenue from the island's cinnamon, they were required to collect and peel the bark as a service obligation. Many later prospered in trade, the professions and public life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salagama.jpg" alt="Salagama" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sarathsilva · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:13th_Century_Salagama_Brahakmana_Flag.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Salagama are also known as Saliya or Chaliya, a name presumably derived from Chale in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. They trace their roots to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel</a> and Malabar coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>, where their ancestors were a weaving community called Saliya, also known as Devanga Chettiar. Like the Karavas and Duravas, other coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, they migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> from South India between the 13th and 18th centuries and settled along the southern coast. Some Salagama bear the vasagama, or surname, Nambudirige, which Gananath Obeyesekere regards as a spurious claim to the status of the Nambudiri Brahmins of Kerala, made in rivalry with the Karava.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Salagama were first described as weavers but turned over time to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">cinnamon</a> peeling. The Heladiva Bamunuwatha chronicle says that Sapumal Kumaraya ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> to do this work in the period 1447–1480. The Portuguese continued to use them as cinnamon workers, who had to provide cinnamon as a tax, and Queyroz lists the Chaleaz among the high castes who prepared it for the great tax. Under Portuguese rule many Salagama converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, which opened the way to education and administrative careers.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1824 counted them as about 7.5 per cent of the coastal Sinhalese population. They were concentrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galle">Galle</a> district, where about half of them lived and where they made up almost 20 per cent of the population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Traditionally the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> was divided into four sub-castes: the Hewapanne, warriors and military officers; the Panividakara, special messengers or headmen; the Kurundukara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">cinnamon</a> workers; and the Uliyakkara. Most Salagama today are Theravada Buddhists, like most Sinhalese. Their home areas around Balapitiya, Kosgoda, Ratgama, Hikkaduwa and Boossa were centres of the anti-caste and anti-colonial movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anagarika-dharmapala">Anagarika Dharmapala</a>, who was not himself a Salagama, and later of the independence struggle, the Lanka Sama Samaja Party and the Communist Party, and they were at the forefront of the Hartal of 1953.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Salagama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Portuguese drew revenue from cinnamon, collected by <b>Salagama</b> peelers as a service obligation, and from areca and elephants.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-ceylon">Portuguese Ceylon</a></li></ul></section>
